
`

we introduced Chemistry Oriented Programming (COP) instead of OOP.

this paradigm inspired by chemistry reaction.

important keys:
-	vessel
-	after
-	solvent
-	solute
-	catalyst
-	its
-	plan
-	elm
-	mix

`

vessel bottle1
vessel bottle2 after bottle1

\ parent class (solvent)
solvent bottle1 Hydrogen: 

	num8 a, num8 _b, num8 c
        bitOff constructor[Hydrogen its, num8 a, num8 b, num8 c]:
		its.a be a
		its._b be b
		its.c be c
        	ok

	ok

\ abstract parent class (catalyst)
catalyst bottle1 Nitrogen: 

	num8 d, num8 _e, num8 f
        bitOff constructor[Nitrogen its]:
		its.d be d
		its._e be e
		its.f be f
        	ok

	\ plans are abstract method
	plan num8 addedBy[Nitrogen its]

	ok

\ interface
solute bottle1 Oxygen: 
	bitOff hello[]
	ok

\ child will inherits all bottle1's
solvent bottle2 Ammonia:

	num8 g, num8 h
        bitOff constructor[Ammonia its, num8 a, num8 b, num8 c]:
		elm.Hydrogen[a, b, c]
		elm.Nitrogen[]
		its.g be 7
		its.h be 9
        	ok


	num8 addedBy[Ammonia its]:
		return its.g + its.h
        	ok

	bitOff hello[]:
		printf ["hello"]
		ok

	ok

\ if you want to mix specific vessel, use 'mix'
solvent bottle2 Water mix [Hydrogen first, Hydrogen second, Nitrogen third]:
	
	num8 g, num8 h
        bitOff constructor[Water its, num8 a, num8 b, num8 c]:
		first[a, b, c]
		second[a, b, c]
		third[]
		its.g be 7
		its.h be 9
        	ok


	num8 addedBy[Water its]:
		return its.g + its.h
        	ok

	ok

\ main
bitOff algo [num8()() args]:
	let substance be Hydrogen[1, 2, 3] == Hydrogen[4,2,3] == Nitrogen [] / '==' keyword is for reaction
	print [substance._atom[Hydrogen, 1].a] / reactions are only have attributes, no longer had methods
	Water x2 be Water[substance] / you can create a substance with reactions, which means adding methods
	Water x1 be Water[1, 2, 9] / or with constructor
	num8 y be x1.first.a
	ok
